## Deferred items

The following were explicitly out of scope for the Phase 1–7
hardening pass.  Each is a candidate for future work but not
load-bearing for current MQC v0 security properties.

### Cosigner-pubkey TOFU bootstrap

Tracked under
[`mtc-keymaster/README-bugsandtodo.md`](../mtc-keymaster/README-bugsandtodo.md)
TODO #9b.  Two design branches drafted in the Claude plans
directory at
`/home/ubuntu/.claude/plans/lets-plan-to-implement-snoopy-rivest.md`
(leaf-bootstrap branch + CA-bootstrap branch).  Neither branch
has shipped.  All MQC code in this directory assumes the
cosigner pubkey is already a trusted local file at
`~/.TPM/ca-cosigner.pem`.

### Protocol-evolution items (would require a new spec draft)

- **PSK / session resumption / 0-RTT.**  Not in MQC v0.  The
  Finished-doesn't-extend-the-transcript decision (issue #4)
  would change if these were added — currently both peers
  zeroize the Finished MAC keys after consuming them once.
- **Exporter keys** (analog of TLS RFC 8446 §7.5).  No current
  caller needs to derive secondary keys from the MQC session.
- **Re-keying / KeyUpdate post-handshake.**  Sequence counters
  are 64-bit; under current load the 2^64 wrap is effectively
  never.  Re-keying would be needed only for >100 PB-per-key
  use cases.
- **Connection IDs / migration.**  MQC has no notion of
  connection migration across IP changes; a peer that moves to
  a new IP simply opens a new connection.  Adding migration
  would require a CID field in every frame's AAD and a
  resumption flow.
- **ML-KEM-768 → larger KEM migration.**  Spec §4 fixes the
  suite at v0.  A v1 with ML-KEM-1024 (Category 5) would be a
  flag-day cutover under the same single-deployment model that
  Phase 1 used.

### Refinements to existing checks

- **Wildcard subjects, IDNA hostnames** in the §10.7
  expected-identity check.  Current rule is case-insensitive
  exact-or-`<host>-` prefix on the verified subject string.
  Wildcards would let a CA cover multiple hostnames under one
  cert; IDNA support would matter for non-ASCII deployments.
  No current caller exercises either.
- **Client puzzles / proof-of-work** in the §11.2 / §12.6 DoS
  defenses.  Reviewer's issue #12 mentioned them as optional;
  the per-IP and per-cert rate limits + pre-crypto length
  filter + fork backpressure proved sufficient in Phase-4
  stress testing.  Would only be needed if attackers learn to
  reach the per-IP cap from many distinct IPs simultaneously.

### Adjacent-protocol work

- **Equivalent runtime-config plumbing for SLC and MQCP.**  The
  `read_config_long` / `[global]` knob layer that Phase 0
  added to MQC has the same shape as what SLC (TLS 1.3) and
  MQCP (post-quantum QUIC) would need.  Each would get its
  own commit, parallel to issue #6a's pattern.

---

## How to revive any deferred item

Pick an item above, decide what it's blocking, and write a
fresh per-item plan.  The Phase 1–7 plan files (now in git
history) are templates for the format: status, what's broken
today, target design, code edits, spec edits, tests, migration
runbook, exit criteria.
